INT. NET SHED - HARBOR DOCK - DAWN

A long timbered shed that smells of tar and brine. Nets hang from rafters like drying ghosts. Sunlight cuts through the cracks in thin gold blades. A kettle sings on a cast-iron stove.

AUGUSTINE MORROW, 60s, a weathered fisherman in an oilskin, sits on an upturned crate with a netting needle, mending a rip. His daughter LOTTE MORROW, 30s, in a fisherman's sweater, sets two tin mugs on a barrel. She pours. Steam rises.

LOTTE
Drink.

AUGUSTINE
I'm working.

LOTTE
You're sulking.

AUGUSTINE
That too.

She puts the mug in his free hand. He sips. The net rustles. Gulls cry outside.

LOTTE
The boat's fine, Pa.

AUGUSTINE
The boat's mortgaged.

LOTTE
So? We mortgage everything. It's tradition.

AUGUSTINE
Your mother would've said --

LOTTE
Mama would've said "Eat your eggs."

He nearly smiles. The kettle whistles. She lifts it.

LOTTE (CONT'D)
I got the loan. Three more seasons.

AUGUSTINE
Where from?

LOTTE
A man in town.

AUGUSTINE
What man?

LOTTE
A man who liked the boat.

Augustine sets down the needle. The tension in the shed changes, thick and slow.

AUGUSTINE
Lotte. Which man?

The door scrapes open. A rectangle of light. DOMINIC SALVATORE, 50s, a long camel coat, polished shoes, and a hat in his hand, steps in, carefully avoiding a coil of rope.

DOMINIC
Morning, Captain.

Augustine stands. The crate falls. The needle skitters across the planks. Lotte steps between them, pours a third mug, and holds it out.

LOTTE
Dominic. Tea?

DOMINIC
Kind of you.

He takes it. Sips. Nods. Looks at Augustine.

DOMINIC (CONT'D)
Your daughter tells me you're a stubborn man.

AUGUSTINE
She tells me you're a thief.

DOMINIC
She hasn't told you I hold the paper on every boat in this harbor.

Augustine's face closes like a door. He grabs the nearest thing, the netting needle, and Dominic does not blink. Lotte seizes her father's wrist. All three freeze, tea steaming, needle poised, the whole shed hanging on a breath.

AUGUSTINE
(quietly)
Get out of my shed.

DOMINIC
(setting down the mug)
Three seasons, Captain. Think on it.

He goes. The door bangs. The nets sway. Lotte lets go of her father's wrist.

LOTTE
You're welcome.

AUGUSTINE
(slowly)
You knew.

LOTTE
I knew.

She picks up the fallen crate, sets it upright, sits. After a long moment, he sits beside her. Neither drinks. The gulls go on screaming.
